WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
Secure SSH automation in Bash is a layered practice.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.080
Start with host trust.

3
00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:17.100
Manage known hosts deliberately, understand how host keys are provisioned, and treat unexpected host key changes as review events rather than inconveniences to bypass.

4
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:19.300
Protect keys and identities.

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:25.720
Use dedicated automation accounts with clear ownership, purpose, scope, and lifecycle.

6
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.740
Avoid shared privileged keys.

7
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:35.280
Separate production access from lower environments where practical, and remove stale keys when workflows change.

8
00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.120
Invoke SSH safely from Bash.

9
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:49.820
Keep command structure visible, use arrays for options, quote values that should remain literal, and validate hosts, users, ports, paths, and target labels before invocation.

10
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.160
Avoid generated command strings and unnecessary re-parsing.

11
00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.120
Keep remote commands controlled.

12
00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:01.820
Prefer narrow, fixed, reviewed remote actions over arbitrary command text.

13
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:10.240
Check SSH and remote command results, use timeouts and retry limits, and fail clearly before downstream steps depend on false success.

14
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.480
Protect transfers and remote state.

15
00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:26.460
Validate source and destination paths, handle ownership and permissions deliberately, stage important changes where practical, avoid accidental overwrites, and log transfer outcomes without exposing sensitive contents.

16
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.780
Finally, operate the system over time.

17
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:34.040
Scope CI/CD credentials and bastion access carefully.

18
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:45.900
Log useful activity, monitor failed and unusual SSH behavior, review authorized keys and sudo rules, and rotate or revoke access as workflows change.

19
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:50.560
Secure SSH automation makes remote work safer, not slower.
